    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d59">

                <pb facs="0228" n="96" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv01/pg96"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">
                    Leahy Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 59 <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">President Truman to Marshal
                            Stalin
                    </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d59-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1945-06-14">14 June 1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>290. …<anchor n="2" xml:id="d59-f2"/>
                </p>
                <p>I have every confidence that a continuation in the future of our friendly
                    understanding cooperation will meet with the same success in preserving peace
                    and international good will as did our common effort in the war against the
                    Nazis.</p>
                <pb facs="0229" n="97" xml:id="pg97"/>
                <p>I am looking forward with much pleasure to meeting you in the near future and
                    discussing fully our common problems.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d59-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d59-f1-content">
                <p>Presumably sent to the United States Naval Attaché, Moscow, via Navy
                    channels.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d59-f2" n="2" xml:id="d59-f2-content">
                <p>For the text of the paragraph omitted here, see <hi rendition="#italic">
                        Stalin’s Correspondence</hi>, vol.
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">ii</hi>, p. 247.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
